{"author":{"id":"337a442880d7106d509ff84f2f7fef51acce8bce88b081d4f4c3f0298b7e7941"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791691534,"nonce":"O8wm81YDtTlIzm5oKRSnj8OpoNs=","message":"Decision: migrate() serializes file databases with an exclusive flock on \u003cresolved db path\u003e.migrate.lock (polled non-blocking, default 600s bound, MigrationLockTimeout with the lock path), held around re-check of pending migrations, pre-migrate backup and apply; a waiter re-runs the schema guard and returns [] with no backup. Schema guard and the deployment-marker refusal run before the lock; in-memory and temp databases take no lock. Lock order outermost first: run lock (C5), console lock (C6), migrate lock; the migrate lock is innermost and migrate() takes no other lock, so no deadlock. CLI and console report the wait via a new on_wait callback. Chose flock over BEGIN IMMEDIATE because the backup and per-migration transactions (and FK-off PRAGMA outside a transaction) cannot sit inside one write transaction. Reason: flock on the shared inode works across PID namespaces, is dropped by the kernel on any crash, and matches the C5 run lock design.","files":null}]}